import AnswerLayout from "../../layouts/AnswerLayout.astro";

const quickAnswer = `Surround sound speaker placement follows Dolby and THX standards based on your listening position. Front left and right speakers: place at ear height, 22–30° off-center from your seat. Center channel: directly above or below the screen at ear height — never higher or lower than 15° from eye level. Surround speakers (5.1): 90–110° to the side of your seat, slightly above ear height (2 feet above). Rear surrounds (7.1): 135–150° behind the listening position at the same height as the side surrounds. Dolby Atmos height speakers: mounted in the ceiling at 45° in front of and behind the listening position, or angled upward from the front left/right positions. Subwoofer: start at a front corner and adjust from there — subwoofer placement is the most room-dependent variable in the system. Correct placement matters more than speaker brand for most rooms.`;

const fullAnswer = `<h2>Front Left, Center, and Right — The Most Important Speakers</h2>
<p>About 70% of a movie's audio plays through the front three speakers. Getting these right is more important than anything else in the system.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Front L/R:</strong> Place at ear height when seated (tweeter at ear level). Angle 22–30° off-center axis. For in-wall speakers, this is typically the left and right edges of the TV wall. For floor-standing or bookshelf, place 3–6 feet from center.</li>
  <li><strong>Center channel:</strong> Directly above or below the TV or screen, within 15° of eye level. This is non-negotiable — dialogue that comes from too high or too low sounds unnatural and fatiguing. For projector screens, we often use an acoustically transparent screen with the center speaker behind it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Surround Speakers — 5.1 Layout</h2>
<p>In a 5.1 system, the two surround speakers go to the sides of the listening position:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Position:</strong> 90–110° from the front-center axis (directly to the side or slightly behind)</li>
  <li><strong>Height:</strong> 2 feet above seated ear height — roughly 6–7 feet off the floor for most rooms</li>
  <li><strong>Aim:</strong> Point them toward the listening position, not at the wall</li>
</ul>
<p>In a living room where speakers go on the side walls, in-wall surrounds at 6–7 feet height work well. In a dedicated theater with rows of seating, dipole or bipole speakers spread sound more evenly across multiple rows.</p>

<h2>Rear Surrounds — 7.1 Layout</h2>
<p>The rear channels in a 7.1 system handle sounds that pass behind you — helicopter flyovers, crowd noise, environmental ambience.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Position:</strong> 135–150° behind the primary listening position</li>
  <li><strong>Height:</strong> Same height as the side surrounds (6–7 feet)</li>
  <li><strong>Note:</strong> In a small room (under 14 feet deep), rear surrounds are often cramped and a well-placed 5.1 sounds better. Add rear channels only if you have the room for them to work.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Dolby Atmos Height Speakers</h2>
<p>Atmos adds overhead sound for a three-dimensional soundstage. Three placement options:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>In-ceiling (best):</strong> Speakers mounted 45° in front of and 45° behind the listening position. For a single row of seats, two pairs (four speakers) give you a proper 7.1.4 layout.</li>
  <li><strong>Upward-firing modules:</strong> Placed on top of the front L/R speakers, angled toward the ceiling. A compromise that works reasonably well in rooms where ceiling installation is not possible.</li>
  <li><strong>Enabled speakers:</strong> Front-firing speakers with an upward-angled driver. The weakest option — works in small rooms, struggles in larger spaces or rooms with vaulted ceilings.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Subwoofer Placement</h2>
<p>Bass is omnidirectional — you cannot hear where a subwoofer is. What you can hear is room modes (bass buildup or cancellation at certain spots). Start with the subwoofer in a front corner and run the receiver's auto-calibration (Audyssey, YPAO, MCACC). If bass is boomy or one-note, move it. The "subwoofer crawl" — putting the sub in your listening seat and walking the room while playing a bass-heavy track until you find the tightest, most even bass, then placing the sub where you stood — is a real technique that works.</p>

<h2>Room Shape Exceptions</h2>
<p>L-shaped rooms, open floor plans, and rooms with glass walls on one side all require adjusted placement. In an open-plan living room, side surrounds often have to go closer to 70–80° because the walls are not where the geometry assumes they will be. A free in-home consultation lets us map the room before specifying any speaker positions.</p>`;

const proTip = `The biggest placement mistake we see is putting surrounds in the ceiling directly above the listening position — this is for Atmos height, not surround channels. Surround speakers belong to the sides and rear at ear-to-slightly-above-ear height. Ceiling surrounds directly overhead give you no left-right localization for side effects and make dialogue feel disembodied.`;

const kzAngle = `Hill Country custom homes frequently have vaulted ceilings of 18–24 feet and open floor plans that extend into kitchens and dining rooms. These spaces require specialized speaker placement — often a combination of in-wall surrounds, distributed ceiling speakers for the overflow area, and precise subwoofer positioning to prevent bass from disappearing into the open space. We measure every room before specifying a single speaker location.`;
